YUME daemon for Debian
======================

The yume-daemon package installs yumed, /etc/yume/yumed.json, seed
/etc/yume/authorized_keys and /etc/yume/auth_keys.meta files, and a hardened
systemd unit named yumed.service.

The service is intentionally disabled and not started on package install.
Before enabling it, provide:

  * /etc/yume/server.crt
  * /etc/yume/server.key
  * at least one client public key in /etc/yume/authorized_keys
  * optional per-key permissions in /etc/yume/auth_keys.meta
  * /etc/yume/obfs.hex, a protected 32-byte admission secret
  * /etc/yume/inner.hex, a separate protected 32-byte inner PSK
  * an ordinary HTTP service on the configured loopback real_backend

The packaged configuration expects the cover backend at
loopback://127.0.0.1:3000.
Start that separately supervised service before yumed, or change real_backend
to another loopback IP literal and port. yumed probes it at startup and fails
closed when it is absent or unhealthy.

Create independent secret files as the account that will run yumed. Each file
must be owned by yume, mode 0600, and contain exactly 64 lowercase hexadecimal
characters with no newline. For example, as root:

  umask 077
  openssl rand -hex 32 | tr -d '\n' > /etc/yume/obfs.hex
  openssl rand -hex 32 | tr -d '\n' > /etc/yume/inner.hex
  chown yume:yume /etc/yume/obfs.hex /etc/yume/inner.hex
  chmod 0600 /etc/yume/obfs.hex /etc/yume/inner.hex

The daemon runs as the locked yume user. Keep private key material readable
only by root and the yume group, for example:

  chown root:yume /etc/yume/server.key
  chmod 0640 /etc/yume/server.key

An anonym CA or delegated private signing key has a stricter contract: when
configured, it must be a regular non-symlink file owned by yume, mode 0600,
and no larger than 64 KiB.

Then check the installed binary and start the service:

  yumed --version
  systemctl start yumed.service

The unit grants CAP_NET_BIND_SERVICE so yumed can bind TCP port 443 without
running as root. It reads /etc/yume but writes only its runtime, state, and log
directories. Use TCP passthrough if a reverse proxy or load balancer sits in
front of yumed; do not terminate TLS in HTTP mode.
